  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \n\nPatchy fog is possible once again Friday night into Saturday morning,\nespecially in the river valleys where more abundant moisture exists. \n\nA deep column of dry air has settled into the region as a broad \nregion of high pressure sags down from the northern Great Lakes. Deep\nnortheasterly flow and mass subsidence results in dry weather, a few\npassing clouds, and seasonably cooler temperatures as we head into \nthe weekend, persisting into early next week. \n\nThe extended pattern looks to become more unsettled, especially mid-\nnext week and beyond. Ensemble guidance suggests a troughing pattern \nmoving across Canada, pulling southerly flow back into CONUS. This \nfeature will help pull moisture from Hurricane Polo up into the \ncentral Great Plains region and translate it east toward the Ohio \nValley late week. Much remains to be determined as to exactly how \nthings will play out, but the bottom line is that the signal for rain\nreturns as we head into October.",
